The Lion, the Witch and the Net Contributor

A forensic journey through the wardrobe of lifetime fiscal accounting

Martin Ollett — Draft discussion paper, September 2026

Abstract

Somewhere beyond the wardrobe lies a remarkable country.

Its citizens are apparently born at working age, fully educated and in perfect health. They immediately obtain well-paid employment, maintain uninterrupted earnings for several decades, require little from the state, retire for precisely the number of years allowed by the spreadsheet, and then vanish shortly after making their final tax payment.

Their children require no schools. Their illnesses occur only at the statistical average. Their pensions arrive from an unspecified dimension. Their share of infrastructure, defence, justice and public administration is apparently waived.

Most remarkably, their entire lifetime relationship with the public finances can be established by examining one payslip.

This paper investigates that country.

The question examined is deliberately narrower than somebody’s social value, economic usefulness or contribution to national output. It concerns fiscal accounting: whether the public receipts attributable to a person exceed the public expenditure attributed to that person across a defined period.

That quantity cannot be inferred from salary alone. It depends on the accounting boundary, earnings history, taxes actually paid, benefits received, public services consumed or allocated, education, healthcare, retirement, children, migration, shared expenditure, prices, time and—where the person is still alive—assumptions about events that have not happened yet.

Nor can a personal fiscal surplus be enlarged by awarding someone the taxes paid by other people merely because their work contributed to those people being economically productive. Wider economic effects can be studied, but they are not identical to personally paid tax.

The wardrobe is optional.

The ledger is not.

