1. Before entering Narnia, define the accounting problem

“Net contributor” sounds like a description of a person.

It is actually the result of an equation.

For an individual (i), define a lifetime fiscal balance over a specified period as:

[
\boxed{
F_i

\sum_t
\left(
R_{i,t}

X_{i,t}
\right)
}
]

where:

* (R_{i,t}) represents public receipts attributed to that person in period (t); and
* (X_{i,t}) represents public expenditure attributed to that person in period (t).

Depending on the model, receipts might include income tax, employee National Insurance, indirect taxes, council tax and explicitly allocated employer taxes.

Expenditure might include cash benefits, State Pension, healthcare, education, social care and some stated allocation of shared public expenditure.

Immediately, a problem appears.

There is no unique individual lifetime fiscal balance until the model specifies what counts, who gets it attributed to, over what period, and in what prices.

A useful accounting system therefore needs several rules:

1. Count every pound once. A tax receipt cannot become the personal contribution of three different people merely because all three helped create the circumstances in which it was paid.
2. Count every cost once. The same hospital salary cannot simultaneously be charged as payroll expenditure and again as an independent patient cost without reconciliation.
3. Do not switch units. Individual, household and family accounts answer different questions.
4. Do not switch periods. One profitable year does not rewrite forty preceding years.
5. Do not switch tax definitions. Personal tax, employer tax and taxes economically associated with employment are not automatically the same thing.
6. State the price basis. Nominal historical pounds, constant-price pounds and discounted present values are different calculations.
7. Separate observation from allocation from projection. A tax payment already made is not epistemically identical to an estimated share of defence spending or a pension expected thirty years from now.
8. Make the total reconcile. If all national receipts and expenditures are supposedly being allocated, their individual allocations should add back to the chosen national totals, apart from amounts explicitly left unallocated.

That last rule is especially useful.

If a methodology distributes £100 of actual public revenue and somehow manufactures £160 of individual “contributions”, it has not discovered £60.

It has discovered double counting.

Gross salary is therefore not a fiscal contribution.

A £75,000 employee has not paid £75,000 to the Treasury.

Likewise, owning a £400,000 house does not constitute a £400,000 contribution to government revenue merely because the number looks magnificent in conversation.

The ledger records transactions and allocated costs.

It is unimpressed by property valuations, job titles and vibes.

