2. First discover what the statistic you are quoting actually measures

One of the easiest ways to manufacture confusion is to take two legitimate statistics answering different questions and pretend they are competing answers to the same one.

Consider two real UK findings.

The Office for National Statistics estimated that in the financial year ending 2024, 53.3% of people lived in households receiving more in cash benefits and benefits in kind than those households paid in direct and indirect taxes. Benefits in kind include services such as healthcare and education. (Office for National Statistics)

That is a household-based, single-year fiscal-incidence measure.

It is not a completed lifetime account for every individual.

Now consider an apparently very different result.

A 2015 Institute for Fiscal Studies analysis of the generation born between 1945 and 1954 estimated that 93% of individuals paid more in personal taxes than they received in social security over their adult lives. But the IFS explicitly stated that this calculation excluded business taxes and excluded benefits from public-service spending. (Institute for Fiscal Studies)

Both figures can be correct.

They are measuring different things.

[
\boxed{
\text{One year} \neq \text{a lifetime}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{Household} \neq \text{individual}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{Social security} \neq \text{all public expenditure}
}
]

The ONS itself explains another important boundary. Its Effects of Taxes and Benefits methodology does not attempt to allocate some government expenditure to individual households where there is no satisfactory basis for doing so. Examples include capital expenditure, defence and the maintenance of law and order. It also does not apportion items such as Corporation Tax or government receipts from public corporations to households. (Office for National Statistics)

Healthcare expenditure in this type of distributional work is also allocated statistically using information on costs and estimated patterns of service use. It is not a stack of individually itemised NHS invoices attached to each respondent. (Office for National Statistics)

That distinction matters.

A statistic can be completely correct within its stated perimeter while being completely incapable of answering a broader question.

If somebody wishes to claim that an individual has paid for their lifetime share of the entire public system, then the things omitted from the source do not disappear.

They become things that still require accounting.

Leaving several government departments outside the calculation is not fiscal self-sufficiency.

It is excellent wardrobe management.

