3. Three questions that keep being smuggled into one sentence

Much confusion disappears if three different concepts are kept apart.

Personal fiscal incidence

How much public revenue is attributed to this person, compared with how much public expenditure is attributed to them?

That is the principal subject of this paper.

Government cash flow

What money entered and left the government sector?

This is where public payroll, procurement, taxes and transfers are recorded.

Wider economic contribution

How did somebody’s work affect production, employment, productivity, other people’s earnings or future tax receipts?

That is a legitimate economic question.

It is not the same question.

A doctor may make an enormous economic and social contribution by restoring people to health and allowing them to return to employment.

An engineer may design machinery allowing hundreds of other people to become more productive.

A teacher may increase pupils’ future earnings.

Those effects can be modelled.

But they do not transform everybody else’s taxes into tax personally paid by the doctor, engineer or teacher.

That would require a different ledger.

And preferably a photocopier.

