4. The “my work created other taxpayers” multiplication trick

Suppose Alice pays £10,000 in tax.

A doctor helped Alice recover from an illness and return to work.

In a causal economic analysis, one could ask how much additional employment, income or tax revenue arose because of that treatment. That requires a counterfactual: what would Alice have earned and paid without it?

Perfectly legitimate question.

Now change the question.

Who paid the £10,000 tax receipt?

Alice did.

If Alice receives £10,000 of credit in her personal tax account and the doctor also receives £10,000 because the doctor’s work enabled Alice to earn it, the personal ledgers now claim £20,000 against one £10,000 Treasury receipt.

Add Alice’s teacher.

£30,000.

Add her employer.

£40,000.

Add whoever built the road she drove to work on.

We are now developing an extraordinarily productive tax system.

Unfortunately, HMRC still received ten grand.

The money did not multiply.

The attribution claims did.

The rule required for a reconciled personal ledger is therefore brutally simple:

[
\boxed{\text{One receipt. One allocation.}}
]

This does not prohibit analysis of indirect fiscal effects.

It prohibits relabelling indirect effects as multiple instances of the same person personally paying the same tax.

Employer taxes provide another example.

They can be recorded separately. They can be associated with the employment relationship. A model can even adopt a stated economic-incidence assumption about where their burden ultimately falls.

What it cannot do is quietly classify the same amount as employer tax, employee tax and somebody else’s tax depending on which column currently requires assistance.

The same discipline applies to costs.

A child’s £1 of education expenditure cannot be charged in full to the child, in full to Parent A and in full to Parent B and then appear as £3 of public expenditure when the government spent £1.

National accounts become surprisingly easy if pounds are allowed to breed.

Unfortunately, pounds have so far declined.

