7. The public-sector payslip: four ledgers enter, one argument leaves

Public-sector employment is where several perfectly legitimate accounting questions are most frequently driven into one another at speed.

The ONS estimated 6.21 million UK public-sector employees in June 2026, representing 18.0% of people in employment. (Office for National Statistics)

So the distinction matters at national scale.

Consider a £75,000 job funded entirely from a public budget.

Using the payroll assumptions above:

[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Gross salary paid} &= £75,000,\
\text{Income Tax + employee NI} &= £20,942.60,\
\text{Net salary after those deductions} &= \boxed{£54,057.40}.
\end{aligned}
]

Every number here is real.

The employee genuinely pays the £20,942.60.

Nothing about working for the public sector deletes their Income Tax payment.

But neither does the tax payment delete the salary expenditure that preceded it.

That is why the question matters.

The personal-tax ledger

The worker receives credit for their own applicable tax payments.

Correct.

The government cash-flow ledger

The government financed the salary and recovered part of the payment through taxation.

Also correct.

The £20,942.60 therefore cannot be described, in a source-of-funds cash-flow analysis, as though it appeared independently of the associated public payroll expenditure.

The Treasury is not a cashback app.

The public-service ledger

The employee was hired to produce something: healthcare, teaching, policing, administration, engineering, defence or another public service.

Their salary is an input into the cost of that service.

If that service cost is then allocated to its users, the original payroll cost must be reconciled with it.

Otherwise, the salary is counted once when government pays the worker and again when the patient’s or pupil’s service cost is entered.

Congratulations.

The same nurse has now cost the Treasury twice without working overtime.

The economic-value ledger

The value created by that employee can exceed, equal or fall below their compensation.

That is an economic question requiring evidence about outputs, alternatives and counterfactuals.

The narrow payroll cash flow cannot answer it.

This distinction matters because saying:

“The government paid £75,000 and recovered £20,942.60 through employee tax”

does not imply:

“The worker provided no service,”

and does not imply:

“The worker’s economic value was only £20,942.60.”

It says exactly what it says.

It describes the cash movements.

That is all.

Employer National Insurance makes the boundary even more important. A public employer may also incur statutory employer NIC, pension costs and other employment costs. Where payments occur between bodies inside a consolidated public-sector boundary, some flows may cancel on consolidation even though they matter in the individual bodies’ accounts.

Again:

state the boundary before announcing the result.

The same reasoning applies to nominally private employment financed by a public contract.

A private company appearing on the payslip does not prove that its revenue came from private consumers.

But public procurement is purchasing goods or services, not simply donating wages to the contractor’s workforce.

So follow the money.

Then follow the service.

Then refrain from adding both twice.

It is less magical, but the accounts survive.

