8. The kidneys have declined to follow the forecast

A lifetime fiscal projection that assumes continued health is conditional on continued health.

This is not a moral statement.

It is arithmetic.

Yet the fictional lifetime taxpayer generally possesses organs manufactured to Treasury specification and guaranteed until shortly after retirement.

Real humans have failed to sign the contract.

A useful scale example comes from NIHR’s evidence review on kidney failure. It reports that in 2023 dialysis cost the NHS about £1.05 billion, with an estimated cost of approximately £34,000 per patient per year. (NIHR Evidence)

Five years at that source-year unit would therefore be:

[
5\times£34,000

\boxed{£170,000}.
]

That is a 2023-cost illustration, not a statement that every dialysis patient generates exactly the same expenditure today.

Now consider the other side of the ledger.

If our hypothetical £75,000 worker loses five earning years entirely, under the 2026–27 payroll illustration the lost Income Tax and employee National Insurance is:

[
5\times£20,942.60

\boxed{£104,713}.
]

One event can therefore alter both sides of the projection:

[
\text{higher expenditure}
\quad+\quad
\text{lower receipts}.
]

That is why healthcare cannot simply be represented by a low average allowance and then left frozen irrespective of what actually happens to the person.

For a completed historical account, actual attributable service use should replace assumptions wherever reliable data exist.

For a projection, uncertainty remains uncertainty.

Multiple illnesses also require sane costing.

Separate disease averages cannot simply be added if they contain overlapping hospital stays, consultations, staffing or overhead.

But the opposite manoeuvre is equally invalid: keeping the original healthy-person surplus intact after the person’s health history has changed beyond recognition.

A salary threshold cannot insure its own conclusion against biology.

The kidneys, regrettably, retain editorial independence.

