12. The rest of the government is still waiting outside the wardrobe

Suppose healthcare, education and pensions have now been entered.

Excellent.

The British state has not finished spending.

A September 2026 House of Commons Library briefing puts total UK public-sector spending in 2026–27 at £1.419 trillion. Its functional breakdown shows major expenditure not only on social protection, health and education but on general public services, economic affairs and numerous other functions. Public expenditure also contains both current and capital spending. (House of Commons Library)

The central problem is attribution.

A person’s appendectomy can be linked reasonably directly to that person.

Their exact lifetime consumption of national defence is less cooperative.

What is one citizen’s personal invoice for the submarine fleet?

How much of a court system belongs to somebody who never enters a courtroom but lives in a society whose contracts and property rights depend on courts existing?

What share of a flood barrier belongs to the person whose home never flooded because the barrier worked?

These are not reasons to pretend the expenditure does not exist.

They are reasons to specify an allocation rule.

Equal per-capita allocation is one option.

Usage-based allocation is another for some services.

Income-weighted allocation could be chosen for some analytical purposes.

Some expenditure might remain explicitly unallocated.

Different choices produce different individual balances.

That is unavoidable.

What is avoidable is hiding the choice.

For scale, if a model allocated only £1,000 of shared expenditure to someone for eighty years:

[
80\times£1,000

£80,000.
]

That is arithmetic, not an estimate that £1,000 is the correct figure.

The point is that apparently modest annual allocation assumptions become material over a lifetime.

