Capital does not get to be bought twice either

Public investment introduces another accounting choice.

A cash-flow model might recognise capital expenditure when money is spent.

An accrual-style framework might allocate consumption of capital over the useful life of an asset.

Those can both be legitimate approaches.

What is less impressive is charging the full purchase price and then charging the full depreciation schedule as though the asset had been built twice.

The bridge remains stubbornly singular.

