13. The reconciliation test

A serious lifetime fiscal model should be capable of answering one deeply irritating question:

Where did every amount come from, where was it allocated, and has it already appeared somewhere else?

Imagine the model allocates all public receipts and expenditures to individuals.

Then, abstracting from explicitly unallocated amounts:

[
\sum_i R_i
]

should reconcile with the chosen total public receipts, and:

[
\sum_i X_i
]

should reconcile with the chosen total public expenditure.

If they do not, the difference needs explaining.

Perhaps some items were deliberately excluded.

Fine.

Perhaps a different government boundary was used.

Fine.

Perhaps national-accounting and cash measures differ.

Fine.

Write it down.

The dangerous version is a system in which £1 of tax becomes £1 of personal contribution for the employee, another £1 for their employer, another £1 for the doctor who kept them working and another £1 for the teacher who educated them, while the actual public accounts stubbornly insist that only £1 arrived.

That model does not have four contributors.

It has one tax receipt and a severe attribution problem.

