14. Checking out: please settle the actual account

A defensible lifetime fiscal calculation requires considerably more information than:

“What do you earn?”

At minimum, the calculation needs a defined accounting boundary, earnings and tax history, public payments received, a method for attributing public services, residence history, retirement treatment and a clear rule governing whether the unit is an individual, household or family.

It also needs a price convention.

Adding £1 paid in 1985 directly to £1 paid in 2026 without adjustment produces a nominal historical total.

That may be useful.

It is not the same thing as a constant-price lifetime calculation.

Discounting future amounts to present value creates another measure again and, if used, should be applied consistently to receipts and expenditure.

There is then the small problem of the future.

For somebody who is still alive, future wages are not observations.

Future tax rates are not observations.

Future health is not an observation.

Future retirement length is not an observation.

Future residence is not an observation.

A lifetime balance calculated for a living person is therefore partly a projection.

It should say so.

A useful presentation separates three classes of entries:

Observed amounts — things that have actually happened.

Allocated amounts — expenditure or revenue assigned through a stated methodology.

Projected amounts — future outcomes dependent on assumptions.

Even after somebody dies, some allocations remain model-dependent because there is no individually metered bill for every public good.

The final historical record can, however, replace most of the biographical guesswork.

Actual earnings replace assumed earnings.

Actual retirement duration replaces assumed retirement duration.

Actual medical history replaces an average-health trajectory where attributable cost information is available.

Actual residence replaces migration assumptions.

Relevant final taxes and estate liabilities can be included according to the chosen boundary.

Death, in other words, finally resolves several variables.

A slightly severe audit process, admittedly.

⸻

15. Conclusion: show the ledger

A person can unquestionably pay more into the public finances than the expenditure attributed to them.

Another person can unquestionably receive more attributable expenditure than they pay.

Neither proposition is controversial.

Establishing which description applies to a particular person across an entire life, however, requires an actual lifetime account.

A current salary does not perform that calculation.

A job title does not perform it.

The words “I pay tax” do not perform it.

The market value of somebody’s house does not perform it.

Nor can the missing revenue be obtained by awarding somebody the taxes paid by patients, pupils, customers, employees or children while allowing those same receipts to remain fully credited in the original taxpayers’ accounts.

Economic contribution can be enormous without being the same quantity as personal tax paid.

Public-sector employment can create enormous value without turning payroll taxation into revenue that arose independently of the payroll expenditure.

Children can become future taxpayers without retroactively deleting their childhood expenditure.

A healthy high earner can develop an expensive illness.

A pensioner can have the audacity to continue existing.

A British child can grow up and pay their taxes somewhere else.

And two people currently earning £75,000 can arrive at that salary through careers whose cumulative tax payments differ by hundreds of thousands of pounds.

That is why “net contributor” is not a salary band.

It is the sign on the bottom of a ledger after the accounting boundary, period, allocations and assumptions have been defined.

The lion may talk.

The witch may perform magic.

The wardrobe may contain an entire parallel universe.

The Treasury still requires its columns to reconcile.

Until they do,

“I pay more in than I take out”

is not a completed calculation.

It is a proposition awaiting its accounts.

Show the ledger.

Leave the wardrobe out of it.
